      {
         "id": "SECTION_introduction",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "<p><h3>What is a Quipu?</h3>A <i>quipu</i> (also written <i>khipu</i>) is a system of knotted cords used in the Andes to record and transmit information. Its origin is very ancient and is linked to the sacred city of Caral, in present-day Peru (<htcite0> ; <htcite1> ; <htcite2>).</p>",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "720f6dfd-4b86-4e07-a887-1010e1041a4d",
                     "text": "Ruta Caral",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2024",
                        "month": "11",
                        "day": "02"
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "03c3743c-d268-4730-ba8e-d36b5c49e781",
                     "text": "Youtube - Caral Perú Oficial",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2024",
                        "month": "11",
                        "day": "02"
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "a7abe0b7-b3bf-44d6-ab4d-e7d32d2586ad",
                     "text": "Moras, Roberto.",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2024",
                        "month": "11",
                        "day": "02"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p class=\"desc\"><img id=\"imgQuipuPanel\" onclick=\"htImageZoom('imgQuipuPanel', '0%')\" class=\"imgcenter\"/><b>Figure 1</b>: Panel at the Caral archaeological site, © ZonaArqueólogicaCaral (<htcite0> ; <htcite1>). Photo taken on <htdate2>, during a visit to the archaeological site of Caral.</p>",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "720f6dfd-4b86-4e07-a887-1010e1041a4d",
                     "text": "Ruta Caral",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2024",
                        "month": "11",
                        "day": "02"
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "03c3743c-d268-4730-ba8e-d36b5c49e781",
                     "text": "Youtube - Caral Perú Oficial",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2024",
                        "month": "11",
                        "day": "02"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": [
                  {
                     "type": "gregory",
                     "year": "2024",
                     "month": "-1",
                     "day": "-1"
                  },
                  {
                     "type": "gregory",
                     "year": "2024",
                     "month": "-1",
                     "day": "-1"
                  },
                  {
                     "type": "gregory",
                     "year": "2017",
                     "month": "6",
                     "day": "13"
                  }
               ],
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "Panel at the Caral archaeological site showing a person holding a quipu and a Yupana board.",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p>A quipu has a main cord from which several pendant cords hang. On each cord, numbers are recorded with knots placed in <b>orders</b> from the bottom upward: the first order holds the units (0 to 9), the next order holds the tens (10 to 99), then the hundreds (100 to 999), and so on. The absence of a knot in an order means zero, each knot adds one unit to that order, and a single order never holds more than nine knots (<htcite0> ; <htcite1>).</p>",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "4f8542c9-f060-4d3c-8a67-971b6d63e1ff",
                     "text": "Ascher, M., & Ascher, R.",
                     "page": "pp. 29 - 32",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2013",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "6e9bef8c-6ec5-4c04-b583-aea8e6bbec5b",
                     "text": "Quesada, Eugenio de",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2016",
                        "month": "08",
                        "day": "04"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p>The <b>order</b> of each knot is what gives it its value. One knot in the first order is worth one, but the same knot in the next order is worth ten, and in the order above that it is worth one hundred. For this reason, order is what allows a quipu to represent a precise number: four knots in the tens order and seven in the units order mean 47, while seven tens and four units mean 74. Keeping the correct order is what makes the number exact (<htcite0>).</p>",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "4f8542c9-f060-4d3c-8a67-971b6d63e1ff",
                     "text": "Ascher, M., & Ascher, R.",
                     "page": "pp. 29 - 32",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2013",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p>To go deeper into how the Incas recorded numbers, we recommend the work of Marcia Ascher and Robert Ascher, <i>Mathematics of the Incas: Code of the Quipu</i> (2013). Pages 29 to 32 explain how the orders are filled with knots. The book is available at <a href=\"https://books.google.com.uy/books?hl=en&lr=&id=h169R-sN0toC\" target=\"_blank\">Google Books</a> (<htcite0>).</p>",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "4f8542c9-f060-4d3c-8a67-971b6d63e1ff",
                     "text": "Ascher, M., & Ascher, R.",
                     "page": "pp. 29 - 32",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2013",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ]
      },
      {
         "id": "SECTION_quipu_text",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "<p><h3>Quipus and writing</h3>For a long time it was thought that the Andes had no writing. Today, several researchers propose that the <i>quipus</i> may have recorded much more than numbers: they may also have kept words and narratives, that is, a form of writing (<htcite0> ; <htcite1> ; <htcite2> ; <htcite3> ; <htcite4>).</p>",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "c2ff41fc-7833-4dc2-8882-eaed12a065b7",
                     "text": "Mann, Charles C.",
                     "page": "pp. 1008",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2005",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "2ebd3420-941b-4f17-b5bc-17bb39ef37a0",
                     "text": "Hyland, S.",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2017",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "b74bf294-c758-4a48-88c9-399ca4f021f6",
                     "text": "Cartwright, M.",
                     "page": "Quipu",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2014",
                        "month": "05",
                        "day": "08"
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "6ff52558-f4fb-49cd-a737-0e2f3863ae7b",
                     "text": "Laurencich Minelli, L.",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2016",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "75ca7e93-6ffd-4303-b21d-b669592dcde3",
                     "text": "Urton, G.",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2019",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p class=\"desc\"><img id=\"imgQuipuPanel2\" onclick=\"htImageZoom('imgQuipuPanel2', '0%')\" class=\"imgcenter\"/><b>Figure 2</b>: Knotted cords of a quipu. Panel at the Caral archaeological site, © ZonaArqueólogicaCaral (<htcite0> ; <htcite1>).</p>",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "720f6dfd-4b86-4e07-a887-1010e1041a4d",
                     "text": "Ruta Caral",
